Strategic forest management planning is commonly formulated as a long-horizon linear program. Let a strategic forest planning model be specified by:
- a set of management units (stands) i = 1,...,N, each with a finite set of silvicultural prescriptions p ∈ P_i (including harvest schedules, thinning regimes, species choice, and rotation length) over a planning horizon T (typically 100–200 years discretized into periods t = 1,...,T);
- decision variables x_{i,p} ≥ 0 representing the area of stand i assigned to prescription p, subject to area constraints Σ_p x_{i,p} = A_i;
- yield coefficients y_{i,p,t} (timber volume by product class), cost coefficients c_{i,p,t}, and price coefficients π_{t} that determine revenues, together with flow/even-flow constraints, ending inventory constraints, and ecological/regulatory constraints;
- an objective such as maximization of expected net present value Σ_t δ^t Σ_{i,p} (π_t y_{i,p,t} − c_{i,p,t}) x_{i,p}.

In the standard deterministic formulation, the parameters (π_t), (y_{i,p,t}), (c_{i,p,t}), as well as the constraint right-hand sides, are treated as known constants. However, in practice these inputs are subject to several qualitatively different kinds of uncertainty, including: (i) timber prices and exchange rates; (ii) future product markets and demand; (iii) growth-and-yield uncertainty in y_{i,p,t}; (iv) catastrophic disturbance processes (fire, insect outbreaks, windthrow/storms), which can be modeled as random losses of standing inventory; and (v) long-term climate-change effects on growth, mortality, and disturbance regimes.

Provide a rigorous mathematical framework that:
1. defines and distinguishes these sources of uncertainty (e.g., as stochastic processes, scenario trees, ambiguity sets, or fuzzy parameters), specifying for each how it enters the model coefficients and constraints;
2. formulates a corresponding stochastic / robust / distributionally robust strategic forest planning model whose solution gives an implementable first-stage prescription policy and recourse adaptation rules;
3. proves that the resulting model is well-posed and that its optimal value converges (in a precise sense) to the true expected optimal value as the discretization of uncertainty is refined;
4. provides a tractable solution algorithm capable of handling problem instances of realistic size (N on the order of 10^4–10^6 stands and T on the order of 10–20 periods), with provable convergence or complexity guarantees.

